## 2. Email Backfill

**Starting point:** 0 of 2,953 directory-visible clinics had an email on file, despite the field existing in the schema and being referenced in `IV_FIELDS_GUIDE.md`.

**Root cause (found via Codex investigation):**
- `crawl_iv_clinics.py`'s live Claude Haiku extraction prompt never asked for `email` — the schema in the docs and the schema actually used by the script had drifted apart.
- Even if it had, `merge_crawl_to_clinics.py`'s `build_enrich_payload()` never read or wrote the email field — it would've been silently dropped before reaching `clinics`.
- Crawl4AI's pipeline excludes nav/header/footer (where contact emails usually live) before generating markdown, and `mailto:` links collapse to just their anchor text ("Email us") in markdown conversion — so even a fixed prompt would have low yield against the existing crawl architecture.

**Fix:**
- Patched both scripts (email now in extraction schema + merge payload) for future crawls.
- Built `scripts/backfill_iv_emails.py` — a one-time, zero-LLM-cost regex backfill. Fetches raw HTML (homepage + `/contact` + `/contact-us`), extracts via regex (mailto hrefs + plain-text pattern), filters junk, prefers same-domain matches, checkpointed/resumable.

**Bugs caught during the run (live, not just dry-run):**
- Regex matched image filenames (`hero@2x.jpg`, `flags@2x.png`) as if they were emails — the TLD pattern is indistinguishable from a file extension pattern. Fixed by rejecting known non-email extensions in the domain position. 2 affected rows found and corrected.
- After full backfill, manual audit of duplicate-email clusters surfaced font-foundry license-attribution emails leaking from `@font-face` CSS (`impallari@gmail.com`, `info@indiantypefoundry.com`, `team@latofonts.com`, `hello@rfuenzalida.com`), plus third-party widget/platform addresses (`support@vagaro.com`, `support@webador.com`, `email@typeform.com`) and literal placeholders (`your@email.com`, `email@email.com`, `user123@email.com`, `hi@mystore.com`, `info@mysite.com`). 13 junk addresses nulled out across ~50 affected rows.

**Result:** 1,741 real emails on file. Most duplicate-email clusters that remain are legitimate — multi-location franchises (The DRIPBaR: 58 locations/1 email, Hydration Room: 36, LIVE Hydration Spa: 23, Vivo Infusion: 14, Next Health: 13, etc.) sharing one corporate contact. **Important for outreach:** dedupe the send list by email, not by clinic row, or these franchises get hit with dozens of duplicate "claim your listing" emails.

**Outstanding:** `info@altamontesprings.4everyoung.com` matches 7 different 4Ever Young franchise locations across unrelated states — unclear if legitimate shared contact or templated default. Excluded from send-list assumption until verified.

## 3. Geographic Expansion

**Finding:** Original Phase 3 Google Places discovery only covered 34 metros. Six states had zero directory-visible clinics (VT, ME, MT, ND, SD, WV); several more were near-zero (AK, NH, PR, WY, DE).

**Action:** Extended `scripts/discover_google_places_gapfill.py` with 17 new metro targets (one per underserved state/territory). Script's resumability (keyed on `discovery_runs` by keyword+city pair) meant the original 34 metros were untouched — confirmed 11 of the 17 ran fresh, while 6 (Boise ID, Des Moines IA, Honolulu HI, Jackson MS, Portland OR, Providence RI) turned out to already have a prior April 25 run, correctly skipped.

**Cost:** $1.76 actual (vs. ~$2.72 estimated) — 55 new (keyword, city) pairs, 288 new `places_discovery` rows, 0 errors.

**Merge:** No reusable merge script existed from the original migration (it was a one-off SQL run, never committed). Built `scripts/merge_places_discovery_pending.py` — processes only `merge_status='pending'` rows, applies quality filter (5+ reviews), closed-business filter, noise filter (hospital/urgent-care/vet/pharmacy keyword patterns), duplicate check against `clinics.google_place_id`, generates slugs matching the exact algorithm in `import-npi-to-supabase.ts` (with real-DB collision checking, not just batch-local).

**Result:** 225 merged, 46 rejected (low review count), 16 rejected (noise), 1 skipped (duplicate). Zero errors.

**Bugs caught post-merge:**
- 23 Puerto Rico clinics had municipality names (Bayamón, Carolina, Guaynabo, etc.) in the `state` column instead of `PR` — Google Places returns the municipio as the state-level address component for PR addresses. Normalized to `PR`.
- 1 US Virgin Islands clinic had the city name in `state`. Normalized to `VI`.
- 3 Canadian businesses (Kemptville ON, Brossard QC, Montréal QC) were merged before being caught — the noise filter checks name/type keywords but not country. Original Phase 3 pipeline had an explicit Canadian-exclusion step; the new merge script doesn't yet. Removed manually; **add country check if gap-fill is run again.**

**Net new clinics:** 222 (225 merged minus 3 removed Canadian).

**Final state count for target states** (data_sources @> ARRAY['google_places_gapfill']): ME 26, PR 23, NH 23, AK 22, ND 21, SD 18, MT 18, WV 17, WY 13, VT 12, DE 12, plus smaller spillover into CO/PA/MN/NY/NJ/FL/VI/IL from search radius overlap.

## Known Gaps / Not Yet Done

1. **The 222 new clinics are unverified.** `is_iv_clinic=true` was set from name/category matching only — same starting state the original 2,945 were in before crawling demoted 645 false positives. These need the same crawl treatment before fully trusting the directory-visible count.
2. **Duplicate detection blind spot:** merge only checked `google_place_id` against existing `clinics` rows. The ~4,415 NPI-seeded rows sitting at `enrichment_status=null` have no `google_place_id`, so a physical clinic discovered by both NPI seeding and this gap-fill could exist as two separate rows. Not yet audited.
3. **Resend DKIM/SPF still not configured** for ivhealthclinics.com — blocks any outbound campaign regardless of email-list readiness.
4. **Hygiene/parity items untouched:** favicon, lead capture ("Request Info") button, canonical audit.

